\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.1}Block diagram}{7}{subsection.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces SoC top level (black and white). Each block reaches the central 32 bit Wishbone bus with three thick arrows: \emph  {data} is bidirectional, while \emph  {control} and \emph  {address} arrows point toward the bus for the two masters (PicoRV32, audio accelerator) and toward the block for the slaves, indicating signal direction. The audio accelerator is both a master (M1, to read the ADC) and a slave (S5, for its control registers), so it reaches the bus with \emph  {two} sets of arrows: the M1 set drawn in the master direction and the S5 set drawn in the slave direction, like every other slave. Inside it the real modules of \texttt  {dma.vhd} are drawn: \texttt  {dma\_proc} (the M1 master) filling the sample BSRAM, \texttt  {fft\_proc} feeding the FFT core and collecting into the result BSRAM. Its spectrum \emph  {drain} does not cross the bus at all, it owns a dedicated write port of the SDRAM controller, which is why the solid drain arrow enters the \emph  {SIP} block directly. The SDRAM port is drawn as its real pieces: the SDRC\_HS SIP (the controller that speaks to the die and owns the write port), the \emph  {fetch FSM} (\texttt  {tst\_} in the RTL), and the two things the bus actually sees as slave S0, the BSRAM snapshot and the \texttt  {fill\_cnt} frame counter. The dashed arrow is the frame done IRQ: in hardware it starts the fetch FSM, which reads the new spectrum back through the SIP, fills the BSRAM and finally bumps \texttt  {fill\_cnt}; the same line also reaches the PicoRV32 as \texttt  {irq[20]}, but the firmware ignores it and polls \texttt  {fill\_cnt} over the bus instead (Section~\ref {sec:sdram}). The thin dashed nets are the clocks, drawn as a distribution tree rooted in the bus column to keep the routing readable: at the bottom the rPLL injects \texttt  {clkout} (40.5\,MHz) and \texttt  {clkoutp} ($+1.54$\,ns) into the spine, and the raw 27\,MHz crystal, tapped before the PLL (the dot on the \texttt  {clk\_i} wire), enters next to it; each block then receives its own labelled branch, \texttt  {clkout} for all the SoC logic, \texttt  {clk\_i} (27\,MHz) for the LED PWM and the GPIO, and \texttt  {clkoutp} only for the SDRAM die, leaving the SoC at the top left. These dashed branches are clock routing only, not Wishbone connections. ITCM and DTCM are CPU local block RAM, not on the Wishbone. The character UART generic slave S6 (\texttt  {0x2}) reaches the ESP32 on pins 17 and 18 and carries the protocol characters plus the \texttt  {\$} diagnostic lines; the pin is driven by this UART alone (the legacy boot banner and hardware decoder drivers are detached). The \textbf  {NOR flash} path now runs through the CPU: the storage commands from the ESP32 land on the S7 UART (pins 72/20, top right), the firmware executes the W25Q sequences on the S8 generic SPI engine (pins 42, 41, 51, 48, left), and the old \texttt  {flash\_ctrl} hardware block is retired (Section~\ref {sec:flashctrl}). The \textbf  {SPI display} slave S9 (\texttt  {spi\_display.vhd}) drives the ST7789 TFT on the five pins freed by the old debug LEDs plus pin 52, through its fixed $\div 6$ prescaler (6.75\,MHz) drawn below the block like every other peripheral divider (Section~\ref {sec:display}). Every external connection carries its real FPGA pin number from the constraints file.}}{7}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:soc}{{2}{7}{SoC top level (black and white). Each block reaches the central 32 bit Wishbone bus with three thick arrows: \emph {data} is bidirectional, while \emph {control} and \emph {address} arrows point toward the bus for the two masters (PicoRV32, audio accelerator) and toward the block for the slaves, indicating signal direction. The audio accelerator is both a master (M1, to read the ADC) and a slave (S5, for its control registers), so it reaches the bus with \emph {two} sets of arrows: the M1 set drawn in the master direction and the S5 set drawn in the slave direction, like every other slave. Inside it the real modules of \texttt {dma.vhd} are drawn: \texttt {dma\_proc} (the M1 master) filling the sample BSRAM, \texttt {fft\_proc} feeding the FFT core and collecting into the result BSRAM. Its spectrum \emph {drain} does not cross the bus at all, it owns a dedicated write port of the SDRAM controller, which is why the solid drain arrow enters the \emph {SIP} block directly. The SDRAM port is drawn as its real pieces: the SDRC\_HS SIP (the controller that speaks to the die and owns the write port), the \emph {fetch FSM} (\texttt {tst\_} in the RTL), and the two things the bus actually sees as slave S0, the BSRAM snapshot and the \texttt {fill\_cnt} frame counter. The dashed arrow is the frame done IRQ: in hardware it starts the fetch FSM, which reads the new spectrum back through the SIP, fills the BSRAM and finally bumps \texttt {fill\_cnt}; the same line also reaches the PicoRV32 as \texttt {irq[20]}, but the firmware ignores it and polls \texttt {fill\_cnt} over the bus instead (Section~\ref {sec:sdram}). The thin dashed nets are the clocks, drawn as a distribution tree rooted in the bus column to keep the routing readable: at the bottom the rPLL injects \texttt {clkout} (40.5\,MHz) and \texttt {clkoutp} ($+1.54$\,ns) into the spine, and the raw 27\,MHz crystal, tapped before the PLL (the dot on the \texttt {clk\_i} wire), enters next to it; each block then receives its own labelled branch, \texttt {clkout} for all the SoC logic, \texttt {clk\_i} (27\,MHz) for the LED PWM and the GPIO, and \texttt {clkoutp} only for the SDRAM die, leaving the SoC at the top left. These dashed branches are clock routing only, not Wishbone connections. ITCM and DTCM are CPU local block RAM, not on the Wishbone. The character UART generic slave S6 (\texttt {0x2}) reaches the ESP32 on pins 17 and 18 and carries the protocol characters plus the \texttt {\$} diagnostic lines; the pin is driven by this UART alone (the legacy boot banner and hardware decoder drivers are detached). The \textbf {NOR flash} path now runs through the CPU: the storage commands from the ESP32 land on the S7 UART (pins 72/20, top right), the firmware executes the W25Q sequences on the S8 generic SPI engine (pins 42, 41, 51, 48, left), and the old \texttt {flash\_ctrl} hardware block is retired (Section~\ref {sec:flashctrl}). The \textbf {SPI display} slave S9 (\texttt {spi\_display.vhd}) drives the ST7789 TFT on the five pins freed by the old debug LEDs plus pin 52, through its fixed $\div 6$ prescaler (6.75\,MHz) drawn below the block like every other peripheral divider (Section~\ref {sec:display}). Every external connection carries its real FPGA pin number from the constraints file}{figure.caption.4}{}}
