\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Address map, decoded from \texttt  {addr[31:27]} (five bits). All ten slaves are live; the NOR flash is reached through two of them (S7 carries the storage commands from the ESP32, S8 is the SPI engine that drives the chip), with the firmware in between. SDRAM sits at \texttt  {0x4800\_0000}, not in the \texttt  {0x0}--\texttt  {0x1} range, because that range is the soft core's own internal space (see text).}}{8}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:addrmap}{{2}{8}{Address map, decoded from \texttt {addr[31:27]} (five bits). All ten slaves are live; the NOR flash is reached through two of them (S7 carries the storage commands from the ESP32, S8 is the SPI engine that drives the chip), with the firmware in between. SDRAM sits at \texttt {0x4800\_0000}, not in the \texttt {0x0}--\texttt {0x1} range, because that range is the soft core's own internal space (see text)}{table.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The 32 bit address is split in two. The top five bits \texttt  {addr[31:27]} pick the slave (Table~\ref {tab:addrmap}); the low 27 bits are the offset inside that slave, of which each peripheral decodes only the few it needs (the per device register maps below).}}{8}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:addrfield}{{3}{8}{The 32 bit address is split in two. The top five bits \texttt {addr[31:27]} pick the slave (Table~\ref {tab:addrmap}); the low 27 bits are the offset inside that slave, of which each peripheral decodes only the few it needs (the per device register maps below)}{figure.caption.6}{}}
