\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Clock tree. The 27\,MHz input is the PLL reference and also clocks the LED PWM and the GPIO directly. The rPLL produces 40.5\,MHz (\texttt  {clkout}) for the CPU, bus and most peripherals, including the speaker PWM (same domain as the bus, so no clock domain crossing on its registers), and a $+1.54$\,ns phase shifted copy (\texttt  {clkoutp}) that clocks only the SDRAM die.}}{11}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:clocktree}{{4}{11}{Clock tree. The 27\,MHz input is the PLL reference and also clocks the LED PWM and the GPIO directly. The rPLL produces 40.5\,MHz (\texttt {clkout}) for the CPU, bus and most peripherals, including the speaker PWM (same domain as the bus, so no clock domain crossing on its registers), and a $+1.54$\,ns phase shifted copy (\texttt {clkoutp}) that clocks only the SDRAM die}{figure.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}SDRAM controller}{11}{section.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:sdram}{{5}{11}{SDRAM controller}{section.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.1}Bring up sequence}{11}{subsection.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Start of an SDRAM read burst at 40.5\,MHz (one clock $=24.7$\,ns). ACTIVATE at T0 opens the row, $t_\mathrm  {RCD}$ later the READ at T3 selects the column, and with CAS latency 3 the first word is valid on DQ at T6 ($3\times 24.7=74$\,ns after the READ). From there the burst continues, one word per clock, until the programmed \texttt  {data\_len} of 26 words is out; then the controller precharges and the row is closed, after which $t_\mathrm  {RP}$ must elapse before the next ACTIVATE (in the figure the burst is cut short to fit the page).}}{12}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sdram-timing}{{5}{12}{Start of an SDRAM read burst at 40.5\,MHz (one clock $=24.7$\,ns). ACTIVATE at T0 opens the row, $t_\mathrm {RCD}$ later the READ at T3 selects the column, and with CAS latency 3 the first word is valid on DQ at T6 ($3\times 24.7=74$\,ns after the READ). From there the burst continues, one word per clock, until the programmed \texttt {data\_len} of 26 words is out; then the controller precharges and the row is closed, after which $t_\mathrm {RP}$ must elapse before the next ACTIVATE (in the figure the burst is cut short to fit the page)}{figure.caption.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.2}From the SDRAM to the CPU: the fetch FSM and the BSRAM snapshot}{12}{subsection.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:fetch}{{5.2}{12}{From the SDRAM to the CPU: the fetch FSM and the BSRAM snapshot}{subsection.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The read path. The accelerator's frame done IRQ does not interrupt the CPU: in hardware it starts the fetch FSM, which reads the 20 row bursts back from the SDRAM and unloads every word into a 512 word BSRAM at its deterministic bin position. When the last burst lands, a frame counter (\texttt  {fill\_cnt}) increments. The CPU polls that counter over the bus and, when it changes, copies the snapshot, which stays frozen for the whole 10.9\,ms frame.}}{13}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fetch-path}{{6}{13}{The read path. The accelerator's frame done IRQ does not interrupt the CPU: in hardware it starts the fetch FSM, which reads the 20 row bursts back from the SDRAM and unloads every word into a 512 word BSRAM at its deterministic bin position. When the last burst lands, a frame counter (\texttt {fill\_cnt}) increments. The CPU polls that counter over the bus and, when it changes, copies the snapshot, which stays frozen for the whole 10.9\,ms frame}{figure.caption.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The fetch FSM. One loop iteration per burst: wait for the controller, strobe the read with the burst's row address, collect words for a fixed 200 cycle window while \texttt  {rd\_valid} unloads into the BSRAM, then move to the next row. After burst 19 it signals the frame and re-arms for the next IRQ.}}{13}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fetch-fsm}{{7}{13}{The fetch FSM. One loop iteration per burst: wait for the controller, strobe the read with the burst's row address, collect words for a fixed 200 cycle window while \texttt {rd\_valid} unloads into the BSRAM, then move to the next row. After burst 19 it signals the frame and re-arms for the next IRQ}{figure.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces S0 slave register map (base \texttt  {0x4800\_0000}). The snapshot reads are registered BSRAM accesses, acknowledged two cycles after the strobe; everything else acks immediately.}}{14}{table.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:s0map}{{5}{14}{S0 slave register map (base \texttt {0x4800\_0000}). The snapshot reads are registered BSRAM accesses, acknowledged two cycles after the strobe; everything else acks immediately}{table.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6}Audio accelerator and tone decoder}{14}{section.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:accel}{{6}{14}{Audio accelerator and tone decoder}{section.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Receive pipeline (black and white). The capture FSM fills one half of the ping-pong block RAM while the FFT FSM processes the other half into a spectrum in SDRAM, through the controller's dedicated write port. The frame done IRQ (dashed) starts the fetch FSM, which copies the spectrum into the stable BSRAM snapshot; the soft core polls the snapshot counter over the bus (S0), copies the 512 bins and, in firmware, decodes one character per tone and sends it to the ESP32.}}{15}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:audio-pipeline}{{8}{15}{Receive pipeline (black and white). The capture FSM fills one half of the ping-pong block RAM while the FFT FSM processes the other half into a spectrum in SDRAM, through the controller's dedicated write port. The frame done IRQ (dashed) starts the fetch FSM, which copies the spectrum into the stable BSRAM snapshot; the soft core polls the snapshot counter over the bus (S0), copies the 512 bins and, in firmware, decodes one character per tone and sends it to the ESP32}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {6.1}Timing in cycles and in seconds}{15}{subsection.6.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {6.2}The two state machines}{16}{subsection.6.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The two parallel FSMs. Capture reads one ADC sample per pass (\texttt  {DS\_OPEN} starts the SPI, \texttt  {DS\_POLL} waits for the conversion, \texttt  {DS\_READ}/\texttt  {DS\_STORE} write it into the ping-pong buffer) and pulses \texttt  {fft\_trigger} every 512 samples. The FFT FSM, on that trigger, feeds the window (\texttt  {FS\_FEED}), runs the FFT (\texttt  {FS\_COMPUTE}), collects the bins (\texttt  {FS\_COLLECT}), then takes ownership of the SDRAM write port and drains them (\texttt  {FS\_SDR\_*}: the settle guard, then warm up plus 20 row bursts) and raises the IRQ (\texttt  {FS\_IRQ}), after which it also streams the frame on a debug pin before re-arming. The two FSMs cannot collide: capture's only shared resource is the Wishbone bus (M1, to reach the ADC), while the drain uses the dedicated SDRAM port and never touches the bus.}}{16}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel-fsm}{{9}{16}{The two parallel FSMs. Capture reads one ADC sample per pass (\texttt {DS\_OPEN} starts the SPI, \texttt {DS\_POLL} waits for the conversion, \texttt {DS\_READ}/\texttt {DS\_STORE} write it into the ping-pong buffer) and pulses \texttt {fft\_trigger} every 512 samples. The FFT FSM, on that trigger, feeds the window (\texttt {FS\_FEED}), runs the FFT (\texttt {FS\_COMPUTE}), collects the bins (\texttt {FS\_COLLECT}), then takes ownership of the SDRAM write port and drains them (\texttt {FS\_SDR\_*}: the settle guard, then warm up plus 20 row bursts) and raises the IRQ (\texttt {FS\_IRQ}), after which it also streams the frame on a debug pin before re-arming. The two FSMs cannot collide: capture's only shared resource is the Wishbone bus (M1, to reach the ADC), while the drain uses the dedicated SDRAM port and never touches the bus}{figure.caption.15}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Audio accelerator control registers, base \texttt  {0x3000\_0000}.}}{16}{table.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {6.3}FFT size and the tone bins}{16}{subsection.6.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Protocol tones, their FFT bins, and which node emits them. Master and slave share the same 46.875\,kHz\,/\,512 grid ($\Delta f = 91.6$ Hz), so every tone is \emph  {on bin} in both directions and a single set of frequencies serves both. The frequencies are the exact bin centres ($f = k\,\Delta f$) used in the code (\texttt  {emit\_tone.c} on the master, \texttt  {global\_parameters.h} on the slave). The two \emph  {carriers} are distinct so each side knows who is speaking; the three \emph  {data} tones (EOF, bit 0, bit 1) are shared by both directions.}}{17}{table.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bins}{{7}{17}{Protocol tones, their FFT bins, and which node emits them. Master and slave share the same 46.875\,kHz\,/\,512 grid ($\Delta f = 91.6$ Hz), so every tone is \emph {on bin} in both directions and a single set of frequencies serves both. The frequencies are the exact bin centres ($f = k\,\Delta f$) used in the code (\texttt {emit\_tone.c} on the master, \texttt {global\_parameters.h} on the slave). The two \emph {carriers} are distinct so each side knows who is speaking; the three \emph {data} tones (EOF, bit 0, bit 1) are shared by both directions}{table.caption.17}{}}
