\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Flash memory map (low 12\,KB). Three 4\,KB sectors: sector 0 holds the 32 byte settings, sectors 1 and 2 hold the 512 slot log ring buffer. Below, the 16 byte record and the settings sector are cut into their byte fields; the debug flag, the TFT theme colours, the view flags and the \texttt  {0x5A} v2 marker are the fields added with the display.}}{21}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flash-map}{{13}{21}{Flash memory map (low 12\,KB). Three 4\,KB sectors: sector 0 holds the 32 byte settings, sectors 1 and 2 hold the 512 slot log ring buffer. Below, the 16 byte record and the settings sector are cut into their byte fields; the debug flag, the TFT theme colours, the view flags and the \texttt {0x5A} v2 marker are the fields added with the display}{figure.caption.23}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {9.2}The state machine, and its firmware mirror}{21}{subsection.9.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces The storage state machine (drawn from \texttt  {flash\_ctrl.vhd}; the firmware port keeps the same shape). At power up it unlocks the chip (WRSR clears the protection bits BP0..BP2 and SRP and sets QE, then a Global Block Unlock), reads the status register back, reports it unsolicited over the UART (\texttt  {'S'} plus the SR byte; in practice the ESP32 boots too late to catch it and asks again with \texttt  {T}), and scans all 512 slots to find the head of the ring buffer, then sits in IDLE. Each UART opcode (\texttt  {L} append, \texttt  {S} save settings, \texttt  {C} clear, \texttt  {G} read slots, \texttt  {H} read head, \texttt  {Q} read settings, \texttt  {I} JEDEC id, \texttt  {T} read status register) is entered from IDLE on its character and returns to IDLE when its SPI sequence finishes; the double headed arrows mean exactly that go and return.}}{22}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flashctrl-fsm}{{14}{22}{The storage state machine (drawn from \texttt {flash\_ctrl.vhd}; the firmware port keeps the same shape). At power up it unlocks the chip (WRSR clears the protection bits BP0..BP2 and SRP and sets QE, then a Global Block Unlock), reads the status register back, reports it unsolicited over the UART (\texttt {'S'} plus the SR byte; in practice the ESP32 boots too late to catch it and asks again with \texttt {T}), and scans all 512 slots to find the head of the ring buffer, then sits in IDLE. Each UART opcode (\texttt {L} append, \texttt {S} save settings, \texttt {C} clear, \texttt {G} read slots, \texttt {H} read head, \texttt {Q} read settings, \texttt {I} JEDEC id, \texttt {T} read status register) is entered from IDLE on its character and returns to IDLE when its SPI sequence finishes; the double headed arrows mean exactly that go and return}{figure.caption.24}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Append (opcode L) as an SPI sequence.}{22}{section*.25}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Append (opcode \texttt  {L}) as the time ordered sequence of SPI frames the controller sends to the W25Q. Each solid box is one CS low frame; the dashed boxes are status polls (read \texttt  {0x05}, loop while the WIP write in progress bit is 1). The controller first probes the target slot; the erase happens only if that slot is not \texttt  {0xFF}, because a NOR program can only turn 1 bits into 0, so writing over a used slot would corrupt it. A read (opcode \texttt  {G}) is instead a single frame: \texttt  {0x03} + 24 bit address, then clock out $N\times 16$ data bytes.}}{22}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flash-append}{{15}{22}{Append (opcode \texttt {L}) as the time ordered sequence of SPI frames the controller sends to the W25Q. Each solid box is one CS low frame; the dashed boxes are status polls (read \texttt {0x05}, loop while the WIP write in progress bit is 1). The controller first probes the target slot; the erase happens only if that slot is not \texttt {0xFF}, because a NOR program can only turn 1 bits into 0, so writing over a used slot would corrupt it. A read (opcode \texttt {G}) is instead a single frame: \texttt {0x03} + 24 bit address, then clock out $N\times 16$ data bytes}{figure.caption.26}{}}
